## CALCULATE FORMULA
what is CALCULATE?

👉 CALCULATE changes the filter conditions and then calculates a value.

CALCULATE(
    COUNTROWS('Hospital ER_Data'),
    'Hospital ER_Data'[Department Referral] <> "None"
)
Read it like English 👇

Count rows
ONLY WHERE
Department Referral is NOT "None"

Input → Output example

Data

Row 1 → Cardiology
Row 2 → None
Row 3 → Neurology


Result

2


(because only 2 rows are not "None")

## SWITCH STATEMENT
I SPENT LOT OF TIME WITH THIS TO UNDERSTAND 
SWITCH WILL BE DONE IN 2 CASES 

CASE1:
🧠 FIRST: The ONE RULE you must understand

SWITCH always compares the FIRST thing with the things that come after it using = (equals)

That’s it. Everything comes from this rule.

🟦 CASE 1: DAY NAME example (NO TRUE())
📋 Table
Day
1
2
3
4
🧮 Code
Day Name =
SWITCH(
    Calendar[Day],
    1, "Monday",
    2, "Tuesday",
    3, "Wednesday",
    "Unknown"
)

🧠 How Power BI thinks (ROW BY ROW)
Row 1 (Day = 1)

Power BI compares:

Calendar[Day] = 1 ?


✅ YES → "Monday"

Row 2 (Day = 2)
Calendar[Day] = 1 ? ❌
Calendar[Day] = 2 ? ✅


→ "Tuesday"

Row 4 (Day = 4)
4 = 1 ? ❌
4 = 2 ? ❌
4 = 3 ? ❌


No match → "Unknown"

✅ Why NO TRUE() here?

Because:

Calendar[Day] is a value

1, 2, 3 are values

SWITCH is happy doing value = value

IN SECOND CASE THERE ARE SOME SITUATIONS IN THAT TIME WE CANT USE == WE WANT > OR < SO IN THAT TIME WE CANT USE > IN PLACE OF == LIKE FIRST CASE SO 
✅ CORRECT VERSION OF YOUR CODE (BEGINNER-SAFE)
Age Group =
SWITCH(
    TRUE(),
    'Hospital er data'[patientage] > 100, "100+",
    'Hospital er data'[patientage] > 90,  "90+",
    "Unknown"
)

🧠 HOW POWER BI EXECUTES THIS (ROW BY ROW)
Example table:
patientage
45
72
105
Row 1: age = 45

45 > 100 → FALSE

45 > 90 → FALSE
👉 Default → "Unknown"

Row 2: age = 72

72 > 100 → FALSE

72 > 90 → FALSE
👉 Default → "Unknown"

Row 3: age = 105

105 > 100 → TRUE
👉 TRUE = TRUE ✅
👉 Result → "100+"

🧠 FINAL GOLDEN RULE (PLEASE REMEMBER THIS)
If you use >, <, >=, <=
→ ALWAYS use SWITCH(TRUE())

If you use exact values (=)
→ use normal SWITCH(column, ...)
